    "created_at": "2019-11-09T00:48:55Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-11-09T00:48:55Z",
    "body": "Sorry, just seeing the mention now…\r\n\r\n> All credit cards that I have seen so far print the expiration time in `MM/YY` format on the cards.\r\n\r\nFWIW, until recently I had a U.S. card with an expiration of MM/DD/YY but I agree that the industry seems to be converging on MM/YY.\r\n\r\n> cc-exp-year is described as the expiration year expressed in YYYY format. As stated above, all credit cards that I have seen use the YY format for the expiration year. This is also common on websites.\r\n\r\nI am opposed to adding `cc-exp-year-yy` as that is already addressed by `<input maxlength=2 autocomplete=cc-exp-year>` which Firefox (and I thought Chrome) already support. Spec text:\r\n> Where it's not possible for the canonical format to be used, user agents should use heuristics to attempt to convert values so that they can be used.\r\n\r\nI would be fine with stronger normative language added to the specification specific to this case (SHOULD or MUST) though.\r\n\r\n> cc-exp-mmyy\r\n\r\nThree thoughts:\r\n1. Is there alignment on what separator character should be used between MM and YY? If not, it may be better to keep pushing authors towards two separate fields with the two existing tokens? I also don't want to add a microsyntax here.\r\n2. I think this could also potentially be addressed by a maxlength of `4` (no separator) or `5` (a single character separator, ideally defined in `pattern` so the browser can brute force some common options) with the existing `cc-exp` which we could add to the spec if we want alignment on it.\r\n  * At quick glance I can't tell if Firefox already does this for filling but we definitely handle saving 2-digit years from `cc-exp`.\r\n3. Do we have reason to believe that sites that are currently affected by this issue (i.e. ones that use a single input and expect YY-MM (or some other separator) would adopt any new token we add? Suggesting `maxlength` and/or `pattern` to these authors seems equally likely to be effective.\r\n\r\n> I haven't checked the historical record, but I would guess that when designing the spec, it was hoped that web authors would ask for YYYY format to get the nice autocomplete. Maybe the spec was designed when Y2K was fresher on everyone's minds.\r\n\r\nI suspect it was to align with `<input type=month>` but in the meantime it has become clear that credit card expiration fields should match what is displayed on the card for best results but that doesn't.",
